From: PS5_Proton <local>
Subject: [PATCH] Host-visible client memory from the application's direct memory

A helper's GPU buffers are shared with the title, and through physical shared memory they were charged to the title's 1 GiB flexible
pool (docs/PLAN.md, B3: two game launchers' GPU processes spiked 200 to 400 MiB there). Direct memory can be shared between
processes when its owner grants the other's pid (sceKernelProtectDirectMemoryForPID, what the system compositor does for games;
dmem-share-2026-10-12-05, dmem-title-share-2026-10-12-01). With ps5_gpu_broker_set_dmem_memory the broker asks the memory helper for
the range (granted to the title and the client), maps it by offset (prot 0x33), and tells the client the offset in the reply's
argument[2] (offset plus one) instead of a descriptor (the client accepts a reply without one); the client maps it at the same address,
and again at the application's address for a placed mapping (vkMapMemory2EXT). On free the client unmaps first, then sends FREE; the
broker unmaps its side and gives the range back to the memory helper, so nothing maps a range the kernel may reuse. A client that is
gone has no mappings, so its ranges are given back when the broker closes it. A range the helper refuses, or this process
cannot map, is made as a shared-memory object instead and the client maps it by descriptor, as before.

Base: PS5_Vulkan 87a48676dda0977d5ba8ff1ddcf02816d41c443b + 0001-0008
